A||N||16||pollock||Hotrodding V6s (long)||||5
Z||000000||pollock||10-26-1999||10:31 AM||||I'm pondering engine swap options.  I think i've settled on sticking with the 60* V6 in some form or another.  (I'm starting with a V6 Fiero).  I have the HPBooks "How to rebuild your GM 60* V6."  <P>After checking out stuff on the internet and in this book, i have a few questions.  The book lists the splayed valve aluminum 3.1 heads as having the most "performance potential."  However, these heads must be used with dished pistons to avoid detonation.  Does that mean the compression ratio will go south when using these pistons?  That's something I want to avoid, but if the pistons are simply dished to make up for smaller combustion chamber volume, that's fine.<BR>Also, the book lists two valve sizes.  It says the splayed valve heads have the larger ones, but so do "high output" 2.8s.  Which size do Fiero motors have?<BR>Yet another, the book says the splayed valve heads use a different intake.  What are the odds that a lower intake from a splayed valve motor will mate with a fiero upper intake?<P>Don't get me wrong-- i have no delusions as to how much more power the splayed valves are worth, i'm just trying to maximize what i can get out of this little motor.<P>My engine-experienced common sense tells me that the GM 3.4 Crate motor is the way to go, but i'm a little underwhelmed by their horsepower claims.  160 hp with the truck induction?  Which means maybe 170 with the Fiero upper end...  Maybe it just needs a more aggressive cam or better heads?  No way would i take this motor apart if i bought it. But i'm inclined to believe you could get more out of it than that.  <P>A few more questions.<BR>How does the stock Fiero torque rating compare with the crate 3.4?<BR>Have any of you done port work on your heads?  Were you happy with the outcome?<BR>Is there a roller cam conversion available for these engines?  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||10-26-1999||11:05 AM||||The aluminum "splayed valve" heads flow better than the cast iron heads, but the Fiero manifold will not fit on to these heads. So, you must use the manifold, injection system, ECM, and wiring harness that was on the donor car too. These 3.1 pistons are specially matched for these aluminum heads and are about 9:1 compression ratio.<P>The Fiero 2.8 heads are the HO cast iron heads with the larger valves. There is a 3.4 cast iron HO head used in some Camaros with a slightly larger exhaust valve than even the Fiero.<P>On the cast iron heads, a little pocket porting is good for increasing flow and adding a few extra ponies. A roller cam, if you can find one, would be a great power investment too. Headers will be needed to complete the better flow formula.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||10-26-1999||05:10 PM||||My latest trip to the junk yard revealed that an 86 Cavalier Z-24 has the aluminum head HO motor with an intake set up for the distributer- the 87+ has DIS systems. If you used this intake on an AL head 3.1, all stock electronics would plug in and work with the stock Fiero computer. This is a win-win situation here. The better heads, higher performance intake system and it is stock ECM compatable. I'm sure that other GM cars in 86 had this same setup, it would be just a matter of looking around for the right intake.||||reg||14
Z||000003||pollock||10-26-1999||06:10 PM||||Wow, that would take a lot of the inconvenience out, and probably get it within the boundaries of "worth the trouble."<P>Do all AL 60 degree heads have the bigger splayed valves?<P>Maybe the better question is, Is there a complete published breakdown on the parts and specs for these motrs?||||reg||7
Z||000004||mwbackus||10-26-1999||08:40 PM||||uh guys, what are ya going to use as an exhaust manifold with your fancy splayed valve heads?  The Fiero manifold wont bolt up to em.<P>BTW, if the hp ratings seem a little low on the crate 3.4, take a close look at the torque ratings, gee, that must be where the power went to, lots and lots of low rpm TORQUE. :-)<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000005||Dave85||10-26-1999||09:05 PM||||Educate me please.  Is the 3.4 crate motor a direct replacement of the 2.8?  If so, how many $$ is it going to run. I too am wanting the most out of my car without doing a major swap. I want the stock look, but more power Arr,Arr,Arr.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cooter||10-26-1999||09:19 PM||||If the Fiero exhaust manifolds won't fit the AL heads, what would be wrong with using the exhaust manifolds from the same engine that the intake came from? I thought the hard part was finding an intake to fit the AL heads and use stock electronics. Surely an exhaust shop could make a pipe to adapt the other manifolds to the fiero exhaust system.||||reg||1
Z||000007||hefty||10-26-1999||09:57 PM||||Will the iron 3.4 HO heads fit on the 2.8 ?||||reg||1
Z||000008||mwbackus||10-26-1999||09:58 PM||||Dave:<BR>The GM Performance S-10 Conversion 3.4 is brand new (no core needed) and is avail for a bit less than $1700.  The starter needs relocation to the other side of the block.  Terryk has done this himself, and would be a good resource for this work.  Ed Parks also offers a complete swap service, including the needed modifcations to the block (he has a drill jig) for a real fair price.  You need to use a neutrally balance flywheel from an 88 V6 (or equiv.), an adj. fuel pressure regulator, 3.4 injectors, a modified pre-88 engine mount, and some other odds and ends that didnt seem like a big deal.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.paceparts.com/ht43.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.paceparts.com/ht43.htm</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#34" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#34</A> <P>Cooter:<BR>For the same reason we cant get any decent damn headers for our cars at a good price.  They exit down instead of back towards the trans and the clearance problems on the firewall side for the stock manifolds used on the aluminum heads would totally prevent this from happening.  If it were at all possible for an exhaust shop to make something like this work, I'd have me some $200 Edelbrook S-10 headers on my Formula in a heartbeat. :-)<P>Hefty:<BR>They are the same heads, so I am sure they would fit just fine.<P>[This message has been edited by mwbackus (edited 10-26-1999).]<p>[This message has been edited by mwbackus (edited 10-26-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cooter||10-26-1999||11:07 PM||||I will look at this car again when I get back to the junk yard. I cant remember how the exhaust ran on this car. So, I need to see if the manifolds direct the exhaust toward the trans instead of down. At my local muffler shop, the owner took three  sets of headers and made one set to fit in/around the frame rails of a small dodge car (I think a Dart) with a blown Hemi in it. The engine was already in the car and the headers were built around it. It was a nightmare of work. But, where there is a will, there is a way. ||||reg||5
Z||000010||batboy||10-27-1999||08:03 AM||||The Cavalier Z-24 engine upgrade sounds interesting. That engine is still a 2.8 though, right?||||reg||1
Z||000011||88 Fiero||10-27-1999||10:39 AM||||MW...I was surfacing thru the web at one time and I ran into another fiero discussion forum..althought this forum is very old about a year ago...anyway...someone mentioned using a 2.8 S10 Truck headers on his fiero and it work fine... he just have to fabricate a crossover pipe...Does anybody thought about going to this route instead of drilling our brains for aftermarket headers???...I have check prices on these S10 Truck 2.8 headers and average price was around $125.00 for the pair..Anyone interested in doing a research on this??? TOM???||||reg||1
Z||000012||mwbackus||10-27-1999||02:03 PM||||Ya, I have heard rumor of this also, thats why I bring up the subject in hopes of someone acknowledging their success with these.  Obviously, if it were really that easy someone would have capitalized on it by now, so I have not pursued the idea to any extent.  Then again, it would not be the first time I have been pleasantly wrong. :-)||||reg||1
Z||000013||Cooter||10-27-1999||02:24 PM||||The z-24 is a 2.8, but its intake shares the same runner spacing as the AL head 3.1. I thought that this would give you 3.1 guys a way to keep the HO aluminum heads and still use the factory electronics. I will keep looking. . .||||reg||6
Z||000014||pollock||10-27-1999||02:27 PM||||mwbackus:  I also noticed the 190 ftlb torque rating of the crate 3.4.  Sounds good, but what is the torque rating on the Fiero 2.8?  Also, why won't the Fiero manifolds go on the splayed valve heads?<P>In general, are we all talking rear wheel or flywheel numbers?  There's usually a big difference, and i'd like to know how they rated the crate motor.  (190hp at the shipping label, hehe....)<P>Back to my original post on this, does anybody know of a roller cam conversion kit for 60 degree V6s?<P>||||reg||14
Z||000015||Phil||10-27-1999||03:53 PM||||Whenever you see HP numbers for crate motors they are crank figures. Every drive train has a different HP drain so they always rate them at the crank with alternator water pump ect. attached.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Tim||10-29-1999||09:20 PM||||GM has a roller cam for small v-6, not for iron head. Would need custom push rods. I forget where I saw it.<BR>||||reg||1
